WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.032
The cost model says: fewer pages, faster queries.

00:00:05.032 --> 00:00:08.065
Hashing got us one IO per lookup.

00:00:08.065 --> 00:00:11.098
Bloom filters skipped IOs entirely.

00:00:11.098 --> 00:00:14.130
There is one more lever.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.163
And it works on every query.

00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:20.195
Make the pages smaller.

00:00:20.195 --> 00:00:23.227
Mickey appears in three of nine Listens tuples.

00:00:23.227 --> 00:00:26.260
At a billion tuples, his user id might appear a hundred million times in a row.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.293
Storing it literally that many times is wasteful.

00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:32.325
Compression exploits the fact that values in a column repeat heavily.

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:35.337
Four ways to shrink a column, same idea.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:38.349
The column repeats, so stop repeating it.

00:00:38.349 --> 00:00:41.362
Pick by what the data looks like.

00:00:41.362 --> 00:00:44.374
First, run-length encoding.

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:47.386
A sorted column with long runs of the same value.

00:00:47.386 --> 00:00:50.398
Instead of writing Mickey a hundred million times, you write Mickey times one hundred million.

00:00:50.398 --> 00:00:53.410
One value plus a count.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.422
Ten-x to a hundred-x smaller, and very fast.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:59.435
Best when the column is sorted with repeated values.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:02.447
Second, dictionary encoding.

00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:05.459
The column has a small set of distinct values.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.471
Genre has maybe fifty.

00:01:08.471 --> 00:01:11.483
Map each distinct value to a small integer code, then store the codes.

00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:14.495
A sixteen-byte string becomes a one-byte number.

00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:17.508
Five-x to twenty-x, fast.

00:01:17.508 --> 00:01:20.520
Best for low-cardinality strings.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.532
Third, delta encoding.

00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:26.544
A sorted numeric column.

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:29.556
Store the difference between consecutive values, not the values themselves.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:32.568
Timestamps microseconds apart turn into tiny deltas that pack into one byte instead of eight.

00:01:32.568 --> 00:01:35.581
Five-x to ten-x.

00:01:35.581 --> 00:01:38.593
Best for sorted numbers and timestamps.

00:01:38.593 --> 00:01:41.605
Fourth, bit packing.

00:01:41.605 --> 00:01:44.617
Small integers do not need thirty-two bits.

00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.629
If the values fit in three bits, store three bits each.

00:01:47.629 --> 00:01:50.641
Four-x to eight-x, very fast.

00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:53.654
Best for small integers and flags.

00:01:53.654 --> 00:01:56.666
One question picks the encoding.

00:01:56.666 --> 00:01:59.678
What does the column look like?

00:01:59.678 --> 00:02:02.132
Real columnar databases stack the techniques.

00:02:02.132 --> 00:02:04.586
Start with the raw user id column.

00:02:04.586 --> 00:02:07.040
Four megabytes.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.495
Run-length encode the sorted runs.

00:02:09.495 --> 00:02:11.949
Two hundred kilobytes.

00:02:11.949 --> 00:02:14.403
Dictionary-encode the values.

00:02:14.403 --> 00:02:16.857
Twenty-five thousand ids fit in a two-byte code instead of a four-byte int.

00:02:16.857 --> 00:02:19.311
One hundred kilobytes.

00:02:19.311 --> 00:02:21.765
Then a general-purpose codec on the leftover bytes.

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:24.220
Fifty kilobytes.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.674
Four megabytes down to fifty.

00:02:26.674 --> 00:02:29.128
Ninety-eight point seven five percent smaller.

00:02:29.128 --> 00:02:31.582
Each layer compounds the last.

00:02:31.582 --> 00:02:34.862
Compression closes the loop on the IO cost model.

00:02:34.862 --> 00:02:38.142
Less data on disk.

00:02:38.142 --> 00:02:41.422
Fewer pages per reed.

00:02:41.422 --> 00:02:44.701
Faster queries.

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:47.981
Modern columnar systems like ClickHouse and Parquet treat compression as a first-class part of their physical storage layer.

00:02:47.981 --> 00:02:51.261
Not an afterthought.

00:02:51.261 --> 00:02:54.541
That is why a columnar warehouse can scan a trillion-tuple table in seconds.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:57.821
The pages are small enough that the disk does not lose.
